  "bio-15c": {
    units: { a: "Cell division and meiosis", b: "Reproduction", c: "Inheritance" },
    paper: "P2",
    qs: [
      /* ---------- c. inheritance ---------- */
      { id: "bio-15-d53", u: "c", s: "A", m: "1", paper: "P1A", t: "A man with blood group A (genotype Iᴬi) and a woman with blood group B (genotype Iᴮi) have a child. What is the probability that the child has blood group O?",
        options: ["0 %", "25 %", "50 %", "75 %"], answer: 1,
        rule: "[1] for the correct option.", pts: ["Answer: B, because each parent passes on i with probability 1/2, so ii = 1/2 × 1/2 = 1/4 = 25 % [1]."] },
      { id: "bio-15-d54", u: "c", s: "A", m: "1", paper: "P1A", t: "A woman who is a carrier of haemophilia (XᴴXʰ) has children with a man who does not have haemophilia (XᴴY). What is the probability that a son of this couple has haemophilia?",
        options: ["0", "1/4", "1/2", "1"], answer: 2,
        rule: "[1] for the correct option.", pts: ["Answer: C, because sons receive their X chromosome from the mother, and half of her X chromosomes carry Xʰ [1]."] },
      { id: "bio-15-d55", u: "c", s: "A", m: "2+2", paper: "P1B", t: "In a hypothetical cross between two tall pea plants, the offspring were 312 tall and 98 short. (a) Calculate the ratio of tall to short offspring. [2] (b) Deduce the genotypes of the parents, with a reason. [2]",
        rule: "M1/A1 in (a); [1] per valid point in (b).", numeric: { value: 3.18, tol: 0.03 },
        pts: ["(a) 312 ÷ 98 [M1]; = 3.18 : 1 [A1].", "(b) Both parents heterozygous Tt [1].", "Short offspring are homozygous recessive tt so each parent must carry t; ratio close to 3 : 1 [1]."] },
      { id: "bio-15-d56", u: "c", s: "A", m: "3", paper: "P2", t: "Explain why the ABO blood group system is an example of both codominance and multiple alleles.", rule: "[1] per valid point, up to [3].",
        pts: ["There are three alleles of the gene: Iᴬ, Iᴮ and i [1].", "Each person has only two of the alleles [1].", "Iᴬ and Iᴮ are codominant so a person with IᴬIᴮ has blood group AB / both antigens expressed [1].", "i is recessive to both [1]."] },
      { id: "bio-15-d57", u: "c", s: "A", m: "2", paper: "P2", t: "Explain how a pedigree chart can show that a genetic condition is caused by a recessive allele.", rule: "[1] per valid point, up to [2].",
        pts: ["Two unaffected parents have an affected child [1].", "So both parents must be carriers / heterozygous [1].", "The condition may skip generations [1]."] },
      { id: "bio-15-d58", u: "c", s: "A", m: "2", paper: "P2", t: "Outline how polygenic inheritance leads to continuous variation, using human height or skin colour as an example.", rule: "[1] per valid point, up to [2].",
        pts: ["Characteristic controlled by several genes [1].", "Effects of alleles are additive so many phenotypes are possible [1].", "Environment also affects the phenotype e.g. diet, sunlight [1].", "Gives a range of phenotypes / normal distribution [1]."] },
      { id: "bio-15-d59", u: "c", s: "C", m: "4+4+7", paper: "P2", t: "(a) Describe the events of mitosis. [4] (b) Distinguish between sexual and asexual reproduction. [4] (c) Explain the inheritance of haemophilia, including how it can be passed from one generation to the next. [7]",
        rule: "[1] per valid point up to the max for each part.",
        pts: ["(a) Prophase: chromosomes condense and nuclear membrane breaks down [1]; spindle forms [1]; metaphase: chromosomes line up at the equator attached to spindle at centromeres [1]; anaphase: sister chromatids separate to opposite poles [1]; telophase: nuclear membranes reform and chromosomes uncoil [1].", "(b) Sexual reproduction involves two parents; asexual one parent [1]; sexual involves meiosis and fusion of gametes [1]; asexual by mitosis [1]; sexual offspring genetically different; asexual offspring clones [1]; asexual is faster / no need to find a mate [1].", "(c) Haemophilia is a disorder where blood does not clot properly [1]; caused by a recessive allele [1]; gene for clotting factor VIII is on the X chromosome / sex-linked [1]; no corresponding gene on the Y chromosome [1]; males XʰY are affected with only one allele [1]; females need two recessive alleles XʰXʰ [1]; carrier females XᴴXʰ are unaffected [1]; carrier mother passes the allele to half of her sons [1]; affected father passes Xʰ to all daughters, who become carriers [1]; so more males than females have haemophilia [1]."] },
    ],
  },
